:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        body = record.getMessage()\n        truncated = _truncate(body, self.max_message_chars)\n        if truncated is not body:\n            record = logging.makeLogRecord? \n```\nCopying the record: `copy.copy(record)` then set msg=truncated, args=(), and handle exc_info/stack_info: if we copy, super().format would append exception text again — that's fine as it's original behavior, and the copy retains exc_info/stack_info. But copying must not affect the original record. copy.copy(record) is shallow — the copy's attributes are set on copy only. super().format on copy sets copy.message. That's safe. But careful: exception formatting uses record.exc_text caching on record; copy would set exc_text on the copy. Fine.\n\nBut the requirement #2 says budget excludes prefixes, exception traceback and stack_info. With the copy approach, we truncate only the message body and let exception text be appended fully. \n\nHowever, is using copy acceptable? \"日志模块只扩展格式化器和默认初始化，可增加必要标准库导入或辅助函数\". copy import is fine. Alternatively use logging.LogRecord reconstruction. copy.copy is cleanest.\n\nWait but there's a subtlety: mutating? copy.copy(record) — shallow copy of LogRecord; fine.\n\nAlternatively avoid copy: temporarily swap msg/args on the record then restore in finally. Restoring works but if an exception occurs... copy is cleaner and safe.\n\nHmm, one concern: does the test check that record.msg/args unchanged? With copy yes unchanged.\n\nAnother concern: check whether they create formatter and check formatted output equals expected truncation with prefix. Using copy, prefix comes from formatting the copy. Good.\n\nAlso `record.message` on the copy is set by super().format. Then multiline handling uses record.message from copy.\n\nLet's write:\n\n```python\n_OMITTED_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, max_chars: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= max_chars:\n        return message\n    remaining = max_chars - len(_OMITTED_MARKER)\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    end = message[len(message) - tail:] if tail > 0 else \"\"\n    return message[:head] + _OMITTED_MARKER + end\n```\n\nCheck example: max 32, marker len 15, remaining 17, head = 9, tail = 8. Matches.\n\nAlso if remaining negative? max_chars>=32 and marker 15 so fine.\n\nValidation:\n\n```python\ndef _validate_max_message_chars(value):\n    if value is None:\n        return None\n    if isinstance(value, bool) or not isinstance(value, int):\n        raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be None or an int >= 32, got {value!r}\")\n    if value < 32:\n        raise ValueError(...)\n    return value\n```\n\nShould non-int like float raise ValueError — yes per spec \"其他值须为大于等于32的整数，布尔值不算整数，否则抛出 ValueError\".\n\nNow default init: `max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`. Need import of environ: check logger.py imports — currently no environ import. Need `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. Careful circular import: environ imports nothing from logger, fine. But logger module being imported by environ? No.\n\nHowever, careful: `_setup_logger()` runs at import time; accessing envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS at import will raise ValueError if env var invalid at import time. In tests, they'd probably set env var then re-run _setup_logger. If invalid value set, accessing raises at import... tests likely set valid values. But maybe a test sets invalid then imports? Risky. Probably fine — spec says \"重新执行默认日志初始化时，将当时的配置值应用到新的格式化器\". So read it inside _setup_logger.\n\nLet me check whether environ imports logger... search for that. Let me read the logger.py lines 33-68 region already. Also check for circular: does deepinfer/environ.py import logging? no.\n\nAlso test may check that independent NewLineFormatter() without arg doesn't read env var — satisfi