    tail = remaining - head\n    ending = message[len(message) - tail:] if tail > 0 else \"\"\n    return f\"{message[:head]}{_OMITTED_MARKER}{ending}\"\n```\n\nThen class:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        fmt: str,\n        datefmt: str | None = None,\n        high_precision: bool = False,\n        use_color: bool = False,\n        *,\n        max_message_chars: int | None = None,\n    ) -> None:\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n        self.max_message_chars = _validate_max_message_chars(max_message_chars)\n```\n\nformat:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            body = record.getMessage()\n            shortened = _truncate_message(body, self.max_message_chars)\n            if shortened != body:\n                record = copy.copy(record)\n                record.msg = shortened\n                record.args = ()\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        ...\n```\n\nWait: `record.getMessage()` may raise if args mismatch, but super().format would raise too. Fine. But calling getMessage then super().format calls getMessage again on the copy — small cost, fine. Actually careful: for the untouched case we don't want double formatting cost... acceptable.\n\nHmm, but `record.getMessage()` on original with args: getMessage does `msg = str(self.msg); if self.args: msg = msg % self.args`. Fine.\n\nAlso note: `super().format(record)` uses record.message = record.getMessage(), and for records with exc_info uses record.exc_info — the copy retains exc_info and stack_info. Good.\n\nEdge: `record.args = ()` — empty tuple is falsy → getMessage won't % again. Good.\n\nAlso copy: need `import copy`. Add to imports list alphabetical: after `import datetime`? order: copy, datetime, logging... Actually `import copy` before datetime.\n\nAlso stack_info: stack text appended after message; kept full — spec says budget doesn't include stack_info. Good.\n\nBut careful: multiline handling uses `record.message`. After copy, record.message set by super().format. Good.\n\nAlso the truncated msg could contain no newline but I need to preserve. Fine.\n\n4. _setup_logger: add max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS.\n\nLet me do the batch edits. Also should I update plan after? Yes, plan update after edits.\n\nLet me write edits carefully with exact current text. I have exact text from evidence for lines 33-68 and 68-96. Let me construct.\n\nEdit A (imports):\nold:\n```\nimport datetime\nimport logging\n```\nnew:\n```\nimport copy\nimport datetime\nimport logging\n```\n\nEdit B (insert marker/helpers before class): old:\n```\nclass NewLineFormatter(logging.Formatter):\n    \"\"\"Adds logging prefix to newlines to align multi-line messages, optionally with microsecond precision.\n```\nnew: helpers + class line + docstring line.\n\nEdit C: __init__ signature and body.\n\nEdit D: format method.\n\nEdit E: _setup_logger fmt = NewLineFormatter( ... ) add param.\n\nLet me write the format replacement using the known exact text:\n\n```\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        msg = super().format(record)\n```\n→\n```\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            body = record.getMessage()\n            shortened = _truncate_message(body, self.max_message_chars)\n            if shortened != body:\n                # 复制记录再改写，避免影响随后交给完整处理器的同一 LogRecord\n                record = copy.copy(record)\n                record.msg = shortened\n                record.args = ()\n        msg = super().format(record)\n```\n\nCareful: `record.getMessage()` where record is typing LogRecord fine.\n\nEnvironment import: also need to ensure `from deepinfe